fix(deploy): own /sessions in the image, so observe_session can write (#211)

Docker initialises an empty named volume from the image's content and ownership
at the mount path. /sessions does not exist in the image, so the daemon created
the mountpoint root-owned and the MCP, which runs as uid 1000, could not write
into it. Every observe_session start refused:

  the session's run was registered and the mapping to it could not be written:
  open /sessions/....part: permission denied

That is the same defect the line above it already carries a paragraph about for
/work, a second time, and it made the tool that moved the session bookkeeping
into the MCP unable to keep any of it. It was found by RM-129's (#208) first
live run of the rewritten harness shim, which is the first caller of the tool
against a real deployment.

Loud rather than silent: a start that cannot write its mapping refuses, and a
subagent with no identity does no work (IP §6.1), so this cost one command to
find rather than a day of runs that stayed Active.

Verified by recreating the volume and restarting the stack: /sessions is now
owned by the image's user, and a full register / six tool calls / retire cycle
through the shim completes with the mapping written and the run retired.

